# Literature review: factors related to mortality in advanced cervical cancer

_Papers reviewed: 3 | Model: muse-spark-1.3-contributor | Cited: 3/3_

This concise review uses only the three provided papers to address factors related to mortality in recurrent, persistent, or metastatic cervical cancer [1][2][3].

Vascular endothelial growth factor (VEGF) promotes angiogenesis, described as a mediator of disease progression in cervical cancer [1]. Bevacizumab, a humanized anti-VEGF monoclonal antibody, is described as having single-agent activity in previously treated, recurrent disease [1]. Most patients developing recurrent cervical cancer are described as having previously received cisplatin with radiation therapy, which reduces the effectiveness of cisplatin at recurrence [1].

In the trial described, 452 patients were randomized using a 2-by-2 factorial design to chemotherapy with or without bevacizumab at 15 mg per kilogram of body weight [1]. Chemotherapy consisted of cisplatin plus paclitaxel or topotecan on days 1 to 3 plus paclitaxel on day 1, repeated every 21 days until disease progression, unacceptable toxic effects, or documented complete response [1]. Improved survival with bevacizumab in advanced cervical cancer is reported in the paper's title [1]. The abstract excerpt provided does not include median survival, hazard ratios, toxicity-related mortality, or subgroup prognostic analyses, so specific mortality effect sizes and independent predictors cannot be determined from the material given [1].

A separate systematic review and meta-analysis aimed to quantify the association of cancer treatment delay with mortality for each 4-week increase in delay to inform treatment pathways [2]. It considered curative, neoadjuvant, and adjuvant surgery, systemic treatment, or radiotherapy for cancers including cervix, as well as bladder, breast, colon, rectum, lung, and head and neck cancers [2]. Delay was measured from diagnosis to first treatment or from completion of one treatment to start of the next, and the primary analysis included only high-validity studies controlling for major prognostic factors, with hazard ratios assumed log-linear and pooled using DerSimonian and Laird random-effects models [2]. The review included 34 studies for 17 indications totaling 1,272,681 patients [2]. The excerpt notes that no high-validity data were found for five of the radi... [text truncated in provided excerpt], and cervix-specific delay-mortality estimates are not provided in the excerpt, so no cervix-specific conclusion about delay and mortality can be drawn from the material given [2].

Annual U.S. estimates projected 1,958,310 new cancer cases and 609,820 cancer deaths in 2023 [3]. For cervical cancer, a 65% drop in incidence during 2012 through 2019 among women in their early 20s, the first cohort to receive human papillomavirus vaccine, is reported as foreshadowing steep reductions in burden [3]. The excerpt does not provide mortality rates, survival outcomes, or prognostic factors specific to advanced cervical cancer [3].

Overall, the provided papers do not support a comprehensive analysis of factors related to mortality in advanced cervical cancer beyond the VEGF/angiogenesis context, reduced cisplatin effectiveness after prior chemoradiation, a reported bevacizumab survival improvement, general treatment-delay methodology, and population incidence context [1][2][3].

Open questions:
1. Does the survival effect of adding bevacizumab to chemotherapy vary by chemotherapy backbone, prior cisplatin-based chemoradiation, or other clinical subgroups [1]?
2. What is the cervix-specific hazard ratio for overall survival per 4-week delay from diagnosis to treatment or between treatments, adjusted for major prognostic factors [2]?
3. What are current population-based mortality and independent prognostic factors for recurrent, persistent, or metastatic cervical cancer, and how has HPV vaccination-related incidence decline affected advanced-disease burden [1][3]?

## Sources

[1] Improved Survival with Bevacizumab in Advanced Cervical Cancer — Krishnansu S. Tewari, Michael W. Sill, Harry J. Long et al. (2014). https://doi.org/10.1056/nejmoa1309748 (score 3.46)
[2] Mortality due to cancer treatment delay: systematic review and meta-analysis — Timothy Paul Hanna, Will D. King, Stéphane Thibodeau et al. (2020). https://doi.org/10.1136/bmj.m4087 (score 3.38)
